# Core Architecture Rule

Use this rule for structural decisions and repo-wide constraints.

## 1) Non-negotiable constraints
- Prefer replacing or deleting flawed code paths over narrow local fixes when a clearer design is available.
- Do not preserve legacy behavior, compatibility shims, or transitional branches unless explicitly requested.
- Keep route rendering, UI orchestration, playback/device services, persistence helpers, and remote adapters separated where practical.
- Prefer stateless, testable utilities and service helpers by default; introduce mutable shared state only when playback lifecycle or device coordination truly requires it.
- Follow standard Go engineering instincts, adapted to TypeScript: simple control flow, explicit names, narrow responsibilities, and comments that preserve intent instead of narrating syntax.
- For non-obvious logic, prefer richer comments that preserve design intent, constraints, invariants, failure modes, trade-offs, and amendment context for future agentic coding sessions.

## 2) Current repository posture
- `src/app/**` owns route entrypoints, page shells, and top-level app integration.
- `src/components/**` should stay focused on rendering and local UI orchestration.
- `src/services/**` should own playback, scheduling, cast handling, and remote/system coordination.
- `src/utils/**` should stay small, dependency-light, and easy to test.
- `src/models/**` should define shared data shapes and domain-facing types.

## 3) Boundary enforcement
- Do not bury playback or cast side effects inside large component render paths when they can live in services or dedicated hooks.
- Keep cross-file assumptions explicit when components, services, and storage helpers rely on the same timing, device, or persistence contract.
- If a change requires coordinated behavior across routing, media playback, and persistence, document the coupling in code comments near the seam.
- Avoid introducing new hidden global mutable state or singleton-style flow ownership without a design note.

## 4) Architecture TBD handling
- Repository-wide architecture guidance is still `TBD by repo owner`.
- Do not invent a new top-level architecture doctrine and present it as settled fact.
- When a task exposes an architectural gap, document the local constraint and leave repo-level direction marked as TBD.
